# Peer key-fingerprint verification (emoji SAS) — cross-platform spec.
#
# Desktop implements this today: src/client/components/security/verify-fingerprint-modal.tsx
# → platform.ts deriveSas → Tauri IPC (apps/desktop/src/crypto.rs derive_sas)
# → packages/crypto/src/sas.rs. Mobile does not: `derive_sas` is not in the
# UniFFI surface (packages/crypto/src/ffi.rs / ffi_v3.rs expose only
# `compute_sas_code`, the 6-digit linking code). The UniFFI export is shared-lane
# work tracked in #751 (iOS) and #761 (Android); these scenarios stay @wip until
# the export lands and each platform wires the ceremony.
#
# ── Shared test vector (proves every platform derives the same emoji) ──
#
# Algorithm (packages/crypto/src/sas.rs `derive_sas`):
#   ikm  = min(pubkeyA, pubkeyB) || max(pubkeyA, pubkeyB) || nonce   (lexicographic order)
#   okm  = HKDF-SHA256(salt = 32 zero bytes, ikm, info = "llamenos:sas-derive:v1", L = 10)
#   emoji[i] = SAS_EMOJI_TABLE[okm[i]]                               (256-entry table, 80 bits)
#
# The vectors below were generated by running the Rust crate itself
# (llamenos_core::sas::derive_sas + sas_emoji). Any platform implementation —
# Rust IPC on desktop, UniFFI on iOS/Android — must reproduce them exactly.
#
#   Vector 1:
#     pubkeyA = abab…ab (32 bytes of 0xAB)
#     pubkeyB = cdcd…cd (32 bytes of 0xCD)
#     nonce   = efef…ef (32 bytes of 0xEF)
#     indices = [106, 40, 91, 43, 165, 81, 126, 9, 221, 64]
#     emoji   = 🚄 🌺 📚 🌴 📰 🛡️ 🗼 🦁 💀 🍊
#
#   Vector 2 (also asserts argument-order independence):
#     pubkeyA = 1111…11 (32 bytes of 0x11)
#     pubkeyB = 2222…22 (32 bytes of 0x22)
#     nonce   = 3333…33 (32 bytes of 0x33)
#     indices = [198, 145, 17, 145, 42, 191, 84, 159, 73, 82]
#     emoji   = ✊ 💎 🐧 💎 🌹 🎒 🔍 🏴 🌶️ 🔒

Feature: Peer key-fingerprint verification (emoji SAS)
  As an admin
  I want to compare an emoji fingerprint with another device's owner
  So that I can confirm their device keys are authentic before trusting them

  # @wip: derive_sas is not in the UniFFI surface; shared-lane export needed first — #751
  @wip
  Scenario: iOS derives the shared SAS emoji vector
    Given the shared SAS test vector from packages/crypto
    When I derive the emoji SAS on iOS for the vector inputs
    Then the 10 emoji should match the Rust-derived sequence exactly
    And swapping the two public keys should produce the identical sequence

  # @wip: derive_sas is not in the UniFFI surface; shared-lane export needed first — #761
  @wip
  Scenario: Android derives the shared SAS emoji vector
    Given the shared SAS test vector from packages/crypto
    When I derive the emoji SAS on Android for the vector inputs
    Then the 10 emoji should match the Rust-derived sequence exactly
    And swapping the two public keys should produce the identical sequence

  # @wip: verify-fingerprint ceremony needs the UniFFI derive_sas export — #751
  @wip
  Scenario: iOS admin verifies a peer device fingerprint
    Given I am authenticated as an admin on iOS
    And I am viewing the device list for a member
    When I tap "Verify" on a peer device
    Then I should see 10 emoji derived from my key, their key, and a fresh nonce
    And requesting a new nonce should change the emoji sequence
    When I confirm the emoji match what the device owner sees
    Then the device should be marked verified through the audit API

  # @wip: verify-fingerprint ceremony needs the UniFFI derive_sas export — #761
  @wip
  Scenario: Android admin verifies a peer device fingerprint
    Given I am authenticated as an admin on Android
    And I am viewing the device list for a member
    When I tap "Verify" on a peer device
    Then I should see 10 emoji derived from my key, their key, and a fresh nonce
    And requesting a new nonce should change the emoji sequence
    When I confirm the emoji match what the device owner sees
    Then the device should be marked verified through the audit API

  # @wip: private-key boundary — assert when the ceremony lands — #751
  @wip
  Scenario: Fingerprint verification never exposes the device private key
    Given the peer fingerprint ceremony is available on mobile
    When I derive the emoji SAS for a peer device
    Then the derivation should execute inside the crypto service layer
    And the device private key should never be returned to the view layer
